#


# Optional directory allowlist. If any "directory:" line is present, every
# repository= and template_dir= used by a device (or a default: line) must
# name one of these directories - by its $TAG or its exact path. This lets a
# less-trusted editor (e.g. a web UI) change devices without being able to
# point them at arbitrary paths. Format: directory: <type> <$TAG> <path>,
# type is "repository", "template", "fetch_run" or "report". If a directory:
# section is present it must cover ALL FOUR types. Remove/omit the whole
# section to disable the allowlist entirely.
#directory: repository $REPO1     /usr/local/fetchconfig/config
#directory: template   $TEMPLATE1 /usr/local/fetchconfig/templates
#directory: fetch_run  $CMD1      /var/fetchconfig/fetch_run
#directory: report     $REP1      /var/www/fetchconfig/reports
# ("directory: fetch_run none" forbids on_fetch_run; "directory: report none"
#  disables change-report writing - each still satisfies its type)

# $Id: device_table.example,v 1.19 2013/10/16 03:29:11 evertonm Exp $
#

# Optional credential aliases (9.80). A "pw:" line names a username or a
# secret once, then referred to by its $Alias on any "default:" or device
# line - the same way a "directory:" line names a $TAG. Keeps repeated
# credentials out of every device line and groups them at the top.
#   pw:  user  $Alias  <username>     (a "user"-kind alias)
#   pw:  pass  $Alias  <secret>       (a "pass"-kind alias)
# The alias name is $ + a letter/underscore then letters/digits/underscore.
# A "user"-kind alias resolves only user-slot options (user, snmp_user);
# a "pass"-kind alias only pass-slot options (pass, enable, snmp_community,
# snmp_write_community, snmp_authpass, snmp_privpass). Username and secret
# obey the normal device_table value rules (quote a value with "," or "=").
#pw:	user	$NetUser	backup
#pw:	pass	$NetPass	"s3cr,et=1"
#pw:	pass	$RoCommunity	public
# then, e.g.:   default: cisco-ios user=$NetUser,pass=$NetPass
#               <model>  sw01  1.1.1.1  snmp_community=$RoCommunity
# A credential value is read as an alias ONLY when it is an unquoted $Name of
# that form; an undefined or wrong-kind $Name is an error, never a literal login.
# To use a LITERAL credential that starts with "$" or "\", escape the first
# character (pass=\$ecret  ->  $ecret; only the first character is de-escaped, so
# DOMAIN\user and C:\tools are untouched) or quote it (pass="$ecret"). A value
# that needs quotes for a "," or space is literal at the same time.
# Review a table's aliases and check every reference resolves with:
#   fetchconfig.pl -devices=FILE --list-credentials

# DEFAULT OPTIONS SECTION
#
#		model		options
#

# Optional: end-of-run backup summary e-mail (independent of any
# model). "user"/"password" are optional -- SMTP AUTH LOGIN is only
# attempted when both are given. "smtp" accepts host or host:port
# (default port 25, plain SMTP, no encryption). May be repeated;
# later values overwrite earlier ones for the same key.
#
# The recipient given here ("to=") always gets the status of every
# device backed up this run. A device can be assigned to a different
# recipient with the device-specific "to=" option (see the DEVICES
# SECTION below); that recipient then gets a separate e-mail limited
# to just their own device(s).
#
#email:		from=config.backup@acme.com,to=admin@acme.com,smtp=mail.acme.com
#email:		from=config.backup@acme.com,to=admin@acme.com,smtp=mail.acme.com,user=svc-fetchconfig,password=xxx
# footer= sets the mail footer's copyright text (" - Version <n>" is
# always appended). Quote the value if it contains a comma; the quotes
# are stripped.
#email:		footer="(c) 2006-2026 Everton da Silva Marques, Rainer Tammer and others"
#
# Change reports (see CHANGE REPORTS in README). write_report=on writes an
# HTML report of what changed to report_dir (a "report" allowlist entry);
# with on and no from=/to= only the report is written, no e-mail is sent.
# email_max_diff diffs are embedded in the mail (default 0 = none). report_logo is a
# file IN report_dir, embedded (250x50 px recommended). report_days>1 prunes
# old reports. mask_secrets (default on) masks common secret forms in diffs.
# report_dir must be group-owned by the web server's group with the setgid
# bit set (chgrp <webgroup> <dir>; chmod g+s <dir>) so fetchconfig-web can
# read/manage the reports fetchconfig writes. See CHANGE REPORTS in README.
#email:		report_dir=$REP1
#email:		write_report=on
#email:		email_max_diff=10
#email:		web_report_url=http://acme.com/cgi-bin/fetchconfig/fetchconfig-web.cgi
#email:		report_logo=logo.png
#email:		report_days=90
#email:		mask_secrets=on
# report_hide: extra custom regex patterns, one per line (accumulate). Only
# the matched substring becomes ****; match just the value (fixed-width
# lookbehind) to keep the keyword. Governed by mask_secrets.
#email:		report_hide=(?<=wpa-passphrase )\S+
# report_hide takes the whole rest of the line verbatim as the regex (no
# comma-split, no quoting, no trimming) - commas, =, spaces are all literal.
#email:		report_hide=(?<=^key )[0-9a-f]{16,}

# Optional: record each run's backup health and config-change history to a
# PostgreSQL database (for a fetchconfig-web dashboard that scales to thousands
# of devices without walking the repository). OFF by default: with no "db:"
# line fetchconfig stays file-based and DBI/DBD::Pg are never loaded. The
# ".status" files are unchanged; the database is a separate, additional sink.
# Fleet-wide, NOT a per-device option. The schema and the writer login role are
# created and owned by fetchconfig-web (run "fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl
# --upgrade"); the engine only writes rows, never issues DDL and never creates
# roles. Nothing blocks the run: each run's records are appended to a per-
# repository write-ahead spool ("<repository>/.db-<pid>-<timestamp>") and, at
# end of run (in the parent, whether or not e-mail is configured), drained into
# the database one transaction per file, oldest first; a file is deleted only
# after its transaction commits. An unreachable database only defers the drain
# to a later run. See README, BACKUP-HISTORY DATABASE.
#   db_inst             database (instance) name (required)
#   db_user             writer login role, default "fetchconfig_dashboard" (required)
#   db_pass             its password; normal device_table quoting, so a value
#                       containing "," or "=" must be double-quoted
#                       (db_pass="a,b=c"). Never written to the log.
#   db_host             "host" or "host:port" (required). IPv6 uses brackets to
#                       add a port, "[2001:db8::1]:5432"; a bare "2001:db8::1"
#                       is the host at the default port (5432).
#   db_connect_timeout  seconds to wait for the connection (default 5); kept
#                       short so a dead database can never stall a run.
#   db_sslmode          libpq sslmode: disable | require | verify-full (default
#                       "disable"; the DB is normally local). Use "require" (or
#                       stronger) across a WAN.
#   db_max_outbox_days  spool (outbox) retention in days (default 90). Spool
#                       files older than this are dropped on a later run.
#db:		db_inst=fetchconfig,db_user=fetchconfig_dashboard,db_pass=secret,db_host=localhost
#db:		db_inst=fetchconfig,db_user=fetchconfig_dashboard,db_pass="a,b=c",db_host=[2001:db8::1]:5432,db_sslmode=require,db_max_outbox_days=120

# Optional: SNMP device inventory (9.80). Model-independent - add the
# "snmp_" options to ANY model's "default:" block or to a device line and,
# in addition to the config backup, fetchconfig collects hardware inventory
# over SNMP (vendor, model, serial, OS version and the per-component serial
# numbers, incl. stack members) and writes it to the PostgreSQL database
# (needs a "db:" line; the three device_inventory* tables are created by
# "fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl --upgrade"). The ".status" file is unchanged.
# Opt-in and OFF by default: inventory runs for a device only when SNMP
# credentials are configured for it - snmp_community (v1/v2c) or snmp_user
# (v3). With no snmp_ option at all the device is simply "not inventoried".
# snmp_inventory itself defaults ON, so setting the community is enough;
# snmp_inventory=off disables a device that would otherwise inherit one.
# Options (defaults in brackets):
#   snmp_community        read community (v1/v2c); its presence enables inventory
#   snmp_write_community  read-WRITE community: arms procurve-snmp's TFTP
#                         trigger, and also enables/serves inventory when
#                         snmp_community is unset (falls back to snmp_community)
#   snmp_version          1 | 2c | 3                               [2c]
#   snmp_port             UDP port                                 [161]
#   snmp_timeout          per-request timeout, seconds             [5]
#   snmp_retries          retries per request                      [1]
#   snmp_max_repetitions  GET-BULK max-repetitions, v2c/v3 (fewer
#                         round-trips on a big stack; lower if an
#                         agent rejects a large bulk reply)         [25]
#   snmp_user             SNMPv3 user; its presence enables inventory (v3)
#   snmp_level            SNMPv3 noAuthNoPriv|authNoPriv|authPriv   [derived]
#   snmp_authproto        SNMPv3 auth protocol (md5|sha|...)        [sha]
#   snmp_authpass         SNMPv3 auth passphrase
#   snmp_privproto        SNMPv3 priv protocol (des|aes|...)        [aes]
#   snmp_privpass         SNMPv3 priv passphrase
#   snmp_inventory        on|off (or 1|0) - master switch          [on]
#   snmp_inventory_level  1 = chassis+modules+PSUs; 2 also optics   [1]
#   snmp_inventory_history on|off (or 1|0) - row on a HW/OS change  [on]
#   snmp_fatal            on|off (or 1|0) - a collection failure
#                         fails the device (exit 11) vs. warning    [off]
#   snmp_inventory_interval  seconds; 0=every run. >0 skips the SNMP
#                         read when the last success is younger than
#                         this and reuses it (keeps backups fast on a
#                         slow-SNMP device, e.g. 86400 = once a day)   [0]
#   snmp_profile_file     override the vendor/model profile file;
#                         DEFAULT:-ONLY - ignored on a device line   [shipped]
# All snmp_* options above may be set on a default: or a device line EXCEPT
# snmp_profile_file, which is valid ONLY on a default: line (a device-line
# value is ignored, with a warning) - it is the one default:-only option.
# The v3 level, if snmp_level is unset, is derived: authpass+privpass =
# authPriv, authpass only = authNoPriv, neither = noAuthNoPriv.
#
# Minimal (v2c) - just the community, applied to a whole model:
#default:	nexus-ssh	snmp_community=public
# Per-device v3, overriding the model default:
#nexus-ssh	nx-sw09	1.1.1.1	snmp_version=3,snmp_user=netmon,snmp_authproto=sha,snmp_authpass=$AuthPass,snmp_privproto=aes,snmp_privpass=$PrivPass

